ed}w(p): & \\text{kilograms} \\\\ p: & \\text{euros per kilogram}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $w(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{kilograms}}{\\text{euros per kilogram}}=\\frac{\\text{squared kilograms}}{\\text{euros}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"squared kilograms per euro\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517201 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1071 "A supplier sells $Q(x)$ items of a product at a price of $x$ euros per item. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9bf87bc-d76d-5ddd-bb05-61b9ce4126ef" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that the quantity $Q(x)$ is measured in items and the input $x$ is measured in euros per item. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x): & \\text{items} \\\\ x: & \\text{euros per item}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ measures a change in $Q(x)$ over a change in $x.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{items}}{\\text{euros per item}}=\\frac{\\text{squared items}}{\\text{euros}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared items per euro\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517181 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1071 "A supplier sells $Q(x)$ meters of a product at a price of $x$ dollars per meter. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/re